---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="South Africa"
      eyebrow="The Cape, the Rand, and a founder cluster"
      dek="Sixty-one Lerenas in a country far from Iberia and the Americas, out of two thousand in the world. That is not scatter. That is a family.">
<section class="blk">
<h2>The founder cluster</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Forebears records <strong>61 bearers of Lerena in South
    Africa</strong>, an incidence of 1 in 888,159. Against a worldwide total of only
    <strong>2,124</strong>, South Africa holds close to <strong>three per cent of every Lerena
    alive</strong> — a country with no Spanish colonial past and no River Plate migration stream to speak of.
  </p>
  <p>
    A rare surname appearing in a country it has no general reason to be in, at that concentration, has one
    ordinary explanation: <strong>one family arrived and multiplied</strong>. That is exactly what the
    family testimony describes. The distribution and the story corroborate each other, and neither was
    derived from the other.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Stated carefully: this makes the South African Lerenas <em>likely</em> to include one large kindred. It
    does not prove that they are <em>all</em> one — see the correction below.
  </p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip">Corrected, 8 September 2026</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      This page originally read the 61 as, in effect, a single family. That was too strong. A deeds register
      of 1927&ndash;47 records the deceased estate of <strong>Lucas Lerena</strong>, whose widow was
      <strong>Rachel Lerenia, born Maleka</strong> — <em>Maleka</em> being a Sotho or Tswana surname — at
      B. Eaton Township. A Pretoria church membership roll of 1939 carries a
      <strong>Hanson Seko Lerena</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    So there are <strong>at least two unconnected Lerena populations in South Africa</strong>: this
    Argentine-descended family of the Cape and the Rand, and one or more <strong>Black South African
    families</strong> bearing the same or a near-identical name. Nothing links them, and this archive
    asserts no link.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founder-cluster reading still holds for <em>this</em> family — the descent from
    <a href={u("/direct-line")}>Pablo Armando Lerena</a> is documented, and the racing dynasty descending
    from him is public record. But the raw figure of 61 cannot be treated as one kindred, and the arithmetic
    that once did so is withdrawn.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The family's own footing here</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented — from the Defranceski archive</span> David Defranceski's MyHeritage
    tree is titled <em>My Family Tree (Defranceski, Lerena, Taylor, Barry, Blazevic, Booyzen)</em>. The
    surnames in that title are themselves evidence:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Booyzen</strong> — an Afrikaans name (also written Booysen, Booyzen, Booyse). Its presence
      means the Lerena line <strong>married into an established Afrikaner family</strong>, and that the
      South African footing was not a brief posting but a settlement.</li>
    <li><strong>Taylor</strong> and <strong>Barry</strong> — English-language names, consistent with the
      Cape and the Rand. <em>Barry</em> is worth a second look: the Barrys were a substantial
      nineteenth-century merchant house in the <strong>Swellendam and Overberg</strong> districts of the
      Cape, in the same world as the Karoo ostrich trade.
      <span class="chip inf">Suggestive only — a common surname, and no link established</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The Defranceski family map records <strong>Johannesburg</strong>
    as the place where David was born and where his father Ivan settled, and then <em>"David Ivan and Cheryl
    move to Brisbane"</em>. The Lerena line therefore reaches Australia <strong>through
    Johannesburg</strong>, not directly from the Americas.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The ostrich farm</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip lore">Family testimony</span> The great-grandfather is believed to have kept an
    <strong>ostrich farm</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a more precise claim than it sounds, because ostrich farming in South Africa was concentrated in
    place and time. The industry centred on <strong>Oudtshoorn</strong> and the <strong>Little Karoo</strong>,
    and the <strong>feather boom</strong> ran from the 1880s to its collapse in <strong>1914</strong>, when
    fashion and the war ended the trade almost overnight. Fortunes were made and lost inside a single
    generation, and the "feather palaces" of Oudtshoorn still stand.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the claim carries a date and a district with it. If it is true, it places the family in the Cape —
    plausibly the Oudtshoorn district — and most likely <strong>before 1914</strong>. That is a testable
    proposition, and a narrow one.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip inf">Now doubtful</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Everything since found points away from it. He was a <strong>Cape Town racing man</strong> — stables
      at Rugby, a training yard taking apprentices, a Newlands address, and "Race Horse Owner" on his death
      certificate — not a Karoo farmer. A search of South African deeds registers returns
      <strong>no land of his at all</strong>: the only Lerena property found is two suburban plots of
      613 and 1,353 square metres belonging to his son <strong>Nuno Fernando</strong> in the 1950s. House
      erven, not a farm.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    That is not disproof. Deeds coverage is partial, an ostrich farm could have been leased rather than
    owned, and Oudtshoorn's records have still not been searched.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip lore">And the family says he did hold land</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <em>"He owned a lot of land up around the <strong>Blouberg</strong> area. We do not know what he
      did."</em> Blouberg is the coast north of Cape Town, beyond Milnerton and Rugby — where his stables
      were. If that is right, then the deeds sweep failed to find land that exists, and the likeliest reason
      is the search rather than the claim: his surname is written <em>Lerena</em>, <em>Lerina</em> and
      <em>Larena</em> across different records. <strong>The registers should be worked again, under every
      spelling, for Blouberg and Milnerton.</strong> <a href={u("/hypotheses")}>Hypotheses &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    An ostrich farmer is also among the occupations the family records for him — <em>"unconfirmed, advised
    by Sue Taylor"</em>. So the tradition has a named source, which is more than it had this morning.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Where the records are</h2>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">NAAIRS — the National Archives</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        South Africa's <strong>National Automated Archival Information Retrieval System</strong> indexes
        estate files, deceased estates, wills and departmental records across the national and provincial
        repositories. A surname this rare should be searchable outright. <strong>Deceased estate files are
        the highest-value target</strong>: they name the widow, the children, the farm and the assets.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Civil registration</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Births, marriages and deaths held by the Department of Home Affairs, with older registers in the
        archives. Cheryl's father's and grandfather's records are the direct route up the line.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Government gazettes</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        For the <em>exequatur</em> recognising a foreign consul — the crispest proof of the consular claim,
        and dated. Union of South Africa gazettes from 1910; Cape Colony gazettes before that.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Newspapers</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The Cape and Rand press reported consular appointments, race meetings and farm sales alike. A single
        social column could carry the name, the post and the farm in one paragraph.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Oudtshoorn and the feather trade</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The <strong>C.P. Nel Museum</strong> at Oudtshoorn and the Cape archives hold the records of the
        feather boom — farm registers, buyers, auction records. If there was a Lerena ostrich farm, this is
        where it is written down.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The Jockey Club and the stud book</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Registrations of owners, breeders and imported bloodstock. See <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The emigration to Australia</h2>
  <p>
    The last leg — Johannesburg to Brisbane — is the best-documented part of the whole line and the easiest
    to close, because it is recent and Australian records are good. It is deliberately left until the living
    generations can be handled properly: <strong>living people are omitted from this build</strong>, and the
    Australian chapter mostly concerns them. <a href={u("/method")}>Method &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The only grave</h2>
  <p class="dek">13 September 2026. The first physical trace of this family found anywhere.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>A <strong>grave kerb</strong> at <strong>Maitland, Woltemade Cemetery</strong> &mdash; poured
      concrete edging, not a headstone &mdash; with one word cut into its face in inset lettering:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:20px 0;text-align:center;padding:26px">
    <div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:2.4rem;letter-spacing:.22em">LERENA</div>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">and nothing else &mdash; no forename, no dates, no epitaph.
      Above the kerb, bare earth and grass.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Maitland Woltemade is where both of them are buried.</strong> Mary Septima in November 1935,
      <strong>grave 7719A</strong>, Roman Catholic, Cemetery no. 1, from the interment register; and Pablo
      Armando on 19 March 1950 &mdash; <em>&ldquo;intended place of burial: Maitland Cemetery no 1&rdquo;</em>
      on his death certificate.</p>
    <p>And it is <strong>the only Lerena grave in the entire eGGSA Western Cape collection</strong>. The
      variants were checked the same afternoon: <strong>LERIN</strong> &mdash; which would catch Lerina and
      Lerini &mdash; returns nothing; <em>Lerena</em> as a substring also covers <em>Llerena</em>, with no
      separate hit; <strong>LAREN</strong> returns thirty-nine records, every one of them Clarence or its
      like.</p>
    <p><strong>It cannot be assigned to a person.</strong> A surname on a kerb, with no dates and no
      forename, is equally consistent with her grave, with his, or with a family plot holding both &mdash;
      and this archive does not turn <em>consistent with</em> into <em>is</em>. What would settle it is the
      grave number: hers is recorded as <strong>7719A</strong>, and the municipal cemetery office holds the
      Woltemade register.</p>
    <p><strong>One small thing it does say.</strong> The stone reads <strong>LERENA</strong> &mdash; which
      runs, lightly, against the hypothesis raised from the DNA that the name might be nearer
      <em>Lerina</em>, the spelling on his death certificate. Not decisive: a kerb may postdate the burial,
      and a mason cuts what he is given. But it is evidence, and it points the other way.
      <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>The photograph is not reproduced here.</strong> It was taken by <strong>Heather
    MacAlister</strong> and published in the <strong>eGGSA Gravestone Photograph Library</strong>, whose
    terms permit limited fair personal use with full credit and forbid replication into another website
    database. So this archive records the facts, which are not copyrightable, and sends you to eGGSA to
    see it &mdash; album <em>&ldquo;Le&rdquo;</em>, image 85 of 127.
    <a href="https://www.graveswcape.eggsa.org/MAITLAND-cemeteries/Maitland-Woltemade/L%20-%20Vanne%20__%20Surnames%20-%20L/Le/IMG_0087.JPG" rel="noopener">See it at eGGSA &#8599;</a>
    <br /><br />
    <strong>And it is not enough.</strong> It is now the first entry on
    <a href={u("/photograph-these/")}>Photograph these</a>, because what is wanted is the whole plot: any
    plate or slab, the section and row markers, and a wide shot showing where it sits.
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
